---
import Site from '../../layouts/Site.astro';
import LibcardExample from '../../components/LibcardExample.astro';
import Icon from '../../../src/components/LibcardIcon.astro';
import '../../styles/libcard.css';
const features = [
  { icon: 'link', title: 'The things you already share.', text: 'Your writing, open-source projects, coaching, and socials can each become a place to direct support. Choose which ones to include.' },
  { icon: 'heart', title: 'One gift. A little direction.', text: 'Supporters choose an amount, make their picks, and review the split. Every tip goes to you, with a suggestion about what they love.' },
  { icon: 'activity', title: 'A signal that travels back.', text: 'Your LibCard can show the share of public picks each link receives. People can discover what your community is excited about.' },
];
const faqs = [
  ['Do all my links become tippable?', 'Only the links and socials you explicitly opt in. The rest stay visible as ordinary destinations. Adding a support action never replaces the link to the work itself.'],
  ['Can someone support me without choosing a project?', 'Yes. The general “Give to” link opens Feedme with nothing selected. Visitors can choose the creator directly with “Just them,” or spread their picks across the work they like.'],
  ['Can I set an aspiration?', 'Yes. Add an optional dollar aspiration to a link in LibCard, or override it in Feedme Studio. It helps describe what more support could make possible. Reaching it is never a condition of receiving tips. LibCard currently shows public pick statistics, not dollar progress toward that aspiration.'],
  ['What happens if one site is temporarily unavailable?', 'Feedme keeps its last successfully imported catalog. If LibCard cannot load public statistics during a build, it leaves out the numbers and keeps the ordinary tip links. A live Feedme server is still needed to complete a checkout.'],
  ['Does this replace Bluesky or Habitat?', 'Your Bluesky or AT Protocol account remains your Feedme identity. LibCard supplies the public profile and link catalog. Feedme keeps its payment ledger and private Habitat recovery; imported LibCard targets are not published as public AT Protocol records in this release.'],
];
---
<Site title="LibCard + Feedme — turn your links into possibilities" description="Connect your LibCard to Feedme so people can tip the work they want more of. Keep your links, your identity, and the freedom to choose what comes next.">
  <section class="libcard-hero wrap">
    <div><p class="eyebrow">LibCard + Feedme</p><h1>Your links.<br/><em>More possibilities.</em></h1><p class="libcard-lead">Let people support the things<br class="libcard-desktop-break"/> they’d love to see more of.</p><p class="libcard-intro">Bring the work you share on LibCard into Feedme. Your community gets a way to encourage it. You get resources and a clearer sense of what resonates.</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button" href="/libcard/setup/">Connect your LibCard <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a class="button secondary" href="#example">See how it works</a></div></div>
    <aside class="libcard-intro-note"><span class="libcard-note-icon" aria-hidden="true"><Icon name="contact" size={28}/></span><h2>Already have a home <br/>for your links?</h2><p>LibCard is an open-source link-in-bio page and virtual business card. Feedme adds a way to support the person behind it.</p><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/crs48/LIBCard">Meet LibCard <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></aside>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap libcard-example-section" aria-label="An example of LibCard and Feedme working together"><LibcardExample/></section>
  <section class="section wrap" aria-labelledby="possibilities-heading"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">A small addition to your everyday page</p><h2 id="possibilities-heading">From “I like this”<br/>to “please, more of this.”</h2></div><div class="libcard-feature-grid">{features.map(feature => <article><span class="libcard-feature-icon" aria-hidden="true"><Icon name={feature.icon} size={23}/></span><h3>{feature.title}</h3><p>{feature.text}</p></article>)}</div></section>
  <section class="libcard-how-band"><div class="wrap libcard-how"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">A familiar path, all the way through</p><h2>Keep the card.<br/>Open a little door.</h2><p>Your titles, icons, links, and GitHub stars stay part of your catalog. Feedme brings the giving flow, with one-time, monthly, and yearly tips.</p></div><ol class="libcard-flow"><li><span>01</span><div><h3>Choose what welcomes support.</h3><p>Opt a link or social into Feedme in your LibCard configuration. Everything else remains an ordinary link.</p></div></li><li><span>02</span><div><h3>Make a few picks.</h3><p>“More of this” opens your Feedme with that item selected. Visitors can add picks to other items or give to you directly.</p></div></li><li><span>03</span><div><h3>Review, then give.</h3><p>Feedme shows the exact split, frequency, privacy choice, and note before sending the visitor to Stripe’s hosted checkout.</p></div></li></ol></div></section>
  <section class="section wrap libcard-signal-section"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Listen to the encouragement</p><h2>Picks tell a story<br/>that dollars alone don’t.</h2><p>A pick is a little extra emphasis. Three picks for a project and one for your writing direct 75% and 25% of that tip. The supporter can change the total amount without losing those choices.</p><p>Across publicly shared, identified, verified tips, LibCard can show where the picks are going. A larger dollar gift doesn’t automatically get a louder voice in this statistic.</p></div><aside class="libcard-signal-card"><p class="eyebrow">Illustrative public signal</p><div class="libcard-signal-value"><strong>75<span>%</span></strong><Icon name="leaf" size={30}/></div><h3>of public picks</h3><p>A share of people’s picks, independent of the dollars they give.</p><div class="libcard-signal-rule"></div><p>Private and anonymous tips stay out of this public signal. Supporter identities, notes, and individual payment amounts aren’t returned to LibCard.</p><small>Numbers refresh when your LibCard rebuilds. They are not a funding-progress meter.</small></aside></section>
  <section class="wrap libcard-ownership" aria-labelledby="ownership-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Two small apps. One coherent experience.</p><h2 id="ownership-heading">Your card stays light.<br/>Your support gets a home.</h2></div><div><h3>LibCard hosts the introduction.</h3><p>Your public profile, links, and opt-ins live in your repository. The card can stay on GitHub Pages with ordinary links and no additional browser JavaScript.</p><h3>Feedme handles the giving.</h3><p>Your own server runs checkout, sign-in, the payment ledger, and private recovery. This static demo site explains the flow; it doesn’t receive real tips.</p><a class="text-link" href="/libcard/setup/">See the setup in both apps <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></section>
  <section class="faq-section wrap"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Good to know</p><h2>A few details.</h2></div><div class="faq-list">{faqs.map(([question, answer]) => <details><summary>{question}<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>{answer}</p></details>)}</div></section>
  <section class="closing wrap"><p class="eyebrow">Make room for more of your work</p><h2>A link to what you do.<br/>A way to help you do it.</h2><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button" href="/libcard/setup/">Read the connection guide <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a class="button secondary" href="/demo/">Explore the Feedme demo</a></div><p class="hero-caption">The demo uses fictional data and takes no payments.</p></section>
</Site>
